<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Barthou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1862–1934</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Barthou was a French politician who was prime minister in 1913 and foreign minister in 1934. He sought to contain Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> through alliances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, including with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and was killed on 9 October 1934 in Marseille during the assassination of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, struck by a stray police bullet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-barthou.jpg" alt="Louis Barthou" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Barthou_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
